<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kenroku-en</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">17th–19th century</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kenroku-en is a landscape garden in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanazawa">Kanazawa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, counted among the Three Great Gardens of Japan. It was developed over the 17th to 19th centuries by the Maeda lords of the Kaga domain beside Kanazawa Castle, with ponds, streams, teahouses and carefully pruned trees. It was opened to the public in 1874 and is now one of the best-known gardens in the country.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kenroku-en.jpg" alt="Kenroku-en" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Japanexperterna.se · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stone_lantern_Kenrokuen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
